hi I'm Scott McCartney travel columnist  at The Wall Street Journal I'm here to  give you some tips on flying to the  other side of the world in a seat like  this and I'll be testing my tips on the  longest airline flight ever taking off  from Newark New Jersey and landing in  Singapore I'm in Newark ready to go on  the longest flight in the world I'm  feeling good now let's see what it's  like when I get there on an aircraft  humidity levels are 20% or less for  reference a comfortable level is 40 to  50 percent this dries out your mouth  skin and everything else it can cause  fatigue headaches bad breath muscle and  joint aches so drink up and think about  your food choices pick items with higher  water content for example Singapore  Airlines has replaced potatoes with  riced cauliflower and one of my favorite  tips brings saline solution to keep your  nose moist and eye drops to keep your  eyes moist put them in a baggie that you  can store in the seat back pocket so you  have easy access tip number two clean  your seat  dehydration dries out the mucous  membranes in your nose which trap  unwanted germs without them you're more  likely to catch things from your fellow  passengers but unlike what you might  think sickness isn't spread through the  airplanes circulating air supply its  surfaces you need to watch out for so  embrace your inner germaphobe and wipe  down your armrest and tray table and  seat back and breathe easier knowing  that modern airplanes have advanced air  filtration systems that trap germs and  bacteria almost to operating room  standards so open that air vent to full  blast and aim it at your nose that's the  cleanest air you're gonna get tip 3 move  depending on your airline you may be  only looking at 31 or 32 inches of space  and coach which is awfully tight for a  long flight  rather than being comfortable get up  frequently if you don't there are side  effects like decrease blood flow which  can increase the chances of blood clots  try sending an alarm on your phone for 3  hours into your flight if you haven't  gotten up and moved yet it's time to  stand drink some tea or coffee well they  will dehydrate you they have the benefit  of being diuretics which will force you  to get up and go to the bathroom and  exercise some airlines including  Singapore are putting exercise videos in  the entertainment system things you can  do while sitting down to increase blood  flow tip 4 adjust your body clock jetlag  starts in the air the cause of this  common problem is that your circadian  rhythm gets mixed up causing fatigue  headaches difficulty concentrating and  even nausea so try setting your watch to  the local time right after takeoff  that'll help you know when to eat and  sleep during the flight on a very long  flight getting at least some sleep is  important and airlines will help with  this by dimming the lights and lowering  the temperature in the cabin at ideal  times for sleep so take their cues just  about everybody to see but sleep is  personal  so do what's best for you for me that's  earplugs an ambien tip 5 limit the booze  alcohol both dehydrates you and messes  with your circadian rhythm this is kind  of a perfect storm for a long flight but  this is one of the pleasures of travel  so take advantage if you want just not  too much  do all that and you should hopefully  walk off the airplane feeling as good as  possible  I must the plane after more than 18  hours legs are a little shaky I didn't  get much sleep pulled an all-nighter but  I'm actually feeling better than I  thought this is the new way of travel  and it's gonna take some adjusting for  people you're gonna have to get in the  right frame of mind to take on this  marathon and you're gonna have to make  some preparations that really will make  a difference  [Music]
 

